{"hash":"e6e88e0d450b10de","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":82.583,"said":"Here sits Nandi, Shiva’s bull, his eyes fixed always on his Lord. The people of Srisailam call him, with affection, Sanagala Basavanna. Stand beside him a while and imagine the temple’s day, for he has watched it unfold countless times. Long before dawn, at about half past three, the Suprabhatam is sung to wake the Lord and the Goddess. At about half past four comes the Maha Mangala Harathi, the great offering of lamps. Lamps of nine different kinds are waved, among them the Nandi, the Naga, the Nakshatra and the Kumbha harathis, until only a single flame remains, the Eka Harathi. In the evening, at the Pradosha hour, the lamps are lit again, and at night the Ekantha Seva lays the deities to rest. Srisailam also keeps something rare among the Jyotirlingas: sparsha darshan, when devotees, at set times and in traditional dress, men in pancha and kanduva and women in saris, may touch the Jyotirlinga itself. Now follow Nandi’s gaze. It leads into the pillared hall ahead, where so many devotees have walked before you.\n\nDid you know? On the busiest days, such as Sundays and Mondays in the month of Kartika, the temple has paused sparsha darshan so that the long queues keep moving.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1028},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1044,"end":1192}],"words":[[0.098,0.325,0,4],[0.435,0.349,5,9],[0.795,0.517,10,15],[1.504,0.481,17,24],[1.997,0.361,25,29],[2.61,0.156,31,34],[2.778,0.264,35,39],[3.055,0.373,40,45],[3.439,0.469,46,52],[3.92,0.132,53,55],[4.064,0.204,56,59],[4.281,0.349,60,64],[5.505,0.108,66,69],[5.625,0.373,70,76],[6.01,0.144,77,79],[6.166,0.673,80,89],[6.851,0.264,90,94],[7.127,0.3,95,98],[7.644,0.156,100,104],[7.812,0.649,105,114],[8.642,0.481,116,124],[9.135,0.649,125,134],[10.745,0.445,136,141],[11.202,0.421,142,148],[11.635,0.168,149,152],[11.815,0.12,153,154],[11.947,0.361,155,160],[12.32,0.204,161,164],[12.536,0.457,165,172],[13